<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>14</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>maxon</AssemblyName>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Auto-detect host RID -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_HostOS Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))">win</_HostOS>
    <_HostOS Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('OSX'))">osx</_HostOS>
    <_HostOS Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Linux'))">linux</_HostOS>
    <_HostArch>
      $([System.Runtime.InteropServices.RuntimeInformation]::OSArchitecture.ToString().ToLower())</_HostArch>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>$(_HostOS)-$(_HostArch.Trim())</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <_IsWindows>$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))</_IsWindows>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="OmniSharp.Extensions.LanguageServer" Version="0.19.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="PublishAfterBuild" AfterTargets="Build">
    <Exec Command="dotnet publish -c $(Configuration) --no-build" />
  </Target>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <_ExeExt Condition="$(_HostOS) == 'win'">.exe</_ExeExt>
    <_ExeExt Condition="$(_HostOS) != 'win'"></_ExeExt>
    <!-- The published compiler's resting place, and the name a LOCKED one is parked under. Both
         targets below name these paths, and they must name the SAME ones: the pre-build removal and
         the post-publish restore are two halves of one contract about one file. -->
    <_BinExe>$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)</_BinExe>
    <_BinExeOld>$(_BinExe).old</_BinExeOld>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ⭐ A FAILED BUILD MUST LEAVE NO COMPILER AT bin/. Publishing and copying are AfterTargets, so a
       build that fails never reaches them and the PREVIOUS bin/maxon.exe survived intact, working,
       and silently one commit (or one branch) out of date. That is not hypothetical: during an arm64
       golden regeneration over ssh, `dotnet` was missing from the non-login shell, `dotnet build`
       failed, `bin/maxon` ran anyway, and the regeneration began minting goldens from the wrong
       compiler. Removing it up front makes the next command fail loudly instead of answering
       confidently from stale code.

       It runs BEFORE the compile, not after a failure, because there is no "after a failure" hook to
       hang it on — and because the property being established is simply that no binary is at that
       path unless THIS build put it there.

       ⚠ Windows cannot delete a RUNNING executable, so a plain Delete is not enough; renaming one is
       allowed, and `.old` is the same parking spot CopyToBin already uses at the other end of the
       build (it clears the previous `.old` first, so the two do not accumulate). If BOTH fail, two
       generations of maxon.exe are running and the build stops rather than proceed toward a copy it
       cannot make.

       ⚠ It deliberately does NOT cover a failure AFTER CopyToBin — `CheckErrorCodeRegistry` runs the
       binary it just published, and a registry that fails the check is not a broken compiler: it is
       the very tool `maxon error-codes generate` needs to fix the registry with. -->
  <Target Name="DiscardStaleBin" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">
    <Delete Files="$(_BinExe)" Condition="Exists('$(_BinExe)')" ContinueOnError="true" />
    <Delete Files="$(_BinExeOld)" Condition="Exists('$(_BinExe)') AND Exists('$(_BinExeOld)')"
      ContinueOnError="true" />
    <Move SourceFiles="$(_BinExe)" DestinationFiles="$(_BinExeOld)"
      Condition="Exists('$(_BinExe)')" ContinueOnError="true" />
    <!-- The MSB3061/MSB3677 warnings above this line name the process holding it, so the message does
         not guess: a build that both diagnoses and misdiagnoses is worse than one that just stops. -->
    <Error Condition="Exists('$(_BinExe)')"
      Text="$(_BinExe) could neither be deleted nor renamed out of the way — the warnings above name what is holding it (a running compiler, plus a previous build's copy parked at $(_BinExeOld) if the rename was blocked too). Stop them, then rebuild: a build that cannot remove the old compiler must not go on to publish over it." />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="CopyToBin" AfterTargets="Publish">
    <!-- Clean up any leftover .old file from a previous locked build -->
    <Delete Files="$(_BinExeOld)" Condition="Exists('$(_BinExeOld)')" ContinueOnError="true" />
    <!-- Try a direct copy first (fast path when not locked) -->
    <!-- On Windows, use Exec instead of Copy to suppress MSB3021 warning when the exe is locked -->
    <Exec
      Command="copy /Y &quot;$(PublishDir)$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; &quot;$(ProjectDir)..\bin\$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot;"
      Condition="$(_IsWindows)" IgnoreExitCode="true" IgnoreStandardErrorWarningFormat="true"
      StandardOutputImportance="low" StandardErrorImportance="low">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_CopyExitCode" />
    </Exec>
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(PublishDir)$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)"
      DestinationFolder="$(ProjectDir)../bin" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" ContinueOnError="true"
      Retries="0" Condition="!$(_IsWindows)">
      <Output TaskParameter="CopiedFiles" ItemName="_CopiedFiles" />
    </Copy>
    <!-- If direct copy failed, rename a LOCKED exe out of the way and copy again. The copy also fails
         when `bin/` itself does not exist (a fresh worktree), and there is nothing to park in that
         case — without the Exists test the Move warns MSB3680 about a source file that was never
         supposed to be there, on exactly the build that has no previous compiler to preserve. -->
    <Move SourceFiles="$(_BinExe)" DestinationFiles="$(_BinExeOld)"
      Condition="Exists('$(_BinExe)') AND (('$(_CopyExitCode)' != '0' AND '$(_CopyExitCode)' != '') OR ('@(_CopiedFiles)' == '' AND '$(_CopyExitCode)' == ''))"
      ContinueOnError="true" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(PublishDir)$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)"
      DestinationFolder="$(ProjectDir)../bin" SkipUnchangedFiles="false"
      Condition="('$(_CopyExitCode)' != '0' AND '$(_CopyExitCode)' != '') OR ('@(_CopiedFiles)' == '' AND '$(_CopyExitCode)' == '')" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE GUARD. docs/error-codes.txt is the single source of truth for the 4-digit
       error-code space; ErrorCode.g.cs and the two Maxon registries are generated from
       it. This fails the build if a number is claimed twice (naming both claimants), or
       if a generated file has drifted from the registry. It runs the compiler we just
       built, so it cannot check a stale binary. -->
  <Target Name="CheckErrorCodeRegistry" AfterTargets="CopyToBin">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; error-codes check &quot;$(ProjectDir)..&quot;" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE SECOND GUARD, and it exists for the same reason as the first: a fact spread across
       files that nothing reconciled. Maxon's top-level namespace is FLAT, so two files of one
       project that export the same name resolve by the order the filesystem lists them — with
       no diagnostic. arm64-linux shipped x86-64's syscall numbers that way, and which numbers
       you got depended on which machine compiled the compiler. This refuses the class in THIS
       repository's own Maxon projects; whether a Maxon PROGRAM may do it is a /specs question
       (A2d) that a compiler diagnostic would decide by accident. -->
  <Target Name="CheckFlatNamespace" AfterTargets="CheckErrorCodeRegistry">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; flat-namespace check &quot;$(ProjectDir)..&quot;" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE THIRD GUARD. The stdlib is parsed PER TARGET (its `#if os(...)` resolve differently) and
       cached keyed by one; get the key wrong and a cross-compile silently inherits the BUILD
       MACHINE's stdlib — `ExitCode` as int(0 to u32.max), Windows path separators — while both
       halves of the compile still succeed and exit 0. There is nothing for a spec test to catch:
       the runner builds ONE target per process, so on any single machine host == target and the
       case passes for the same reason it passed before the fix. This runs the compiler we just
       published, cross-compiling for two targets in one process, so it cannot check a stale binary
       and it is the only place in the build where a second target is ever asked for.

       It costs ~3.2 s (measured 2026-07-31: 3.52/3.11/3.25 s over three runs) against a ~48-71 s
       `dotnet build` — call it 5%, for the only check that exercises the cross-compile path at all.

       AFTER the registry and flat-namespace checks, chained to the latter so the order is DEFINED rather than two targets sharing one anchor: a drifted registry is the cheaper, more common failure
       and wants to be the first thing you see, and unlike this one it is fixable by a command
       (`maxon error-codes generate`) rather than by a code change. Like that target, this leaves the
       published binary in place on failure — a compiler that fails this IS broken, but it is also
       the only compiler you have to debug it with, and the non-zero build is what says so. -->
  <Target Name="CheckStdlibTargetCache" AfterTargets="CheckFlatNamespace">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; stdlib-target-selftest" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE FOURTH GUARD, and the third guard's twin: that one says the cross-compile is CORRECT, this
       one says its output may not be COMMITTED as a golden. A golden is worth what validated it, and
       the only thing that validates one here is the test having RUN — which a foreign-OS binary cannot
       do on this host, because `maxon-sharp` launches every test with Process.Start and owns no runner,
       emulator or VM. Like the third guard there is nothing for a spec case to catch: the corpus is
       only ever run natively, so the refusing arm is never taken on any machine that runs it.

       It costs one process launch and compiles nothing - the rule is a pure function of the host and
       the requested target. Chained AFTER the stdlib check, so the four guards stay a defined sequence
       rather than two of them sharing an anchor. -->
  <Target Name="CheckGoldenMintHost" AfterTargets="CheckStdlibTargetCache">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; golden-mint-selftest" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE FIFTH GUARD, and the fourth's other half. The fourth says a foreign-OS target's goldens may
       not be MINTED here; this one says what a test that could not be RUN here is worth. They read the
       same one fact (`TargetRunHost.WhyCannotRun`) and this guard deliberately does NOT re-check its
       rows - it checks the VERDICT the fact feeds, which is its own: NotRunHere when the host never
       could have launched the binary, and a plain FAILURE when it should have. The second direction is
       the dangerous one to lose, because excusing every failed launch would wave through a binary this
       compiler had just emitted and could not execute.

       Nothing a spec case can reach, again: a case cannot ask which host it is on, and the corpus is
       only ever run natively. It compiles nothing and launches nothing - every row is a pure function
       of a synthesized launch result - so it costs one process. Chained AFTER the mint guard, keeping
       the five a defined sequence rather than two sharing an anchor. -->
  <Target Name="CheckSpecRunVerdict" AfterTargets="CheckGoldenMintHost">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; spec-run-selftest" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE SIXTH GUARD, and the only one here that protects the USER'S SOURCE rather than this
       compiler's own behaviour. `maxon fmt` rewrites files IN PLACE, and for an unknown length of time
       it silently DELETED COMMENTS: measured 2026-08-20, one run over a pristine copy of
       `maxon-shv2/Compiler/Runtime/GtRuntime.maxon` destroyed 338 comment lines, exit 0, reporting only
       `changed: true`. Nothing in the tree could have caught it, because the formatter had no test of
       any kind - and `.claude/CLAUDE.md` recommended the tool by name.

       It pins CONTENT, not layout: every comment survives, and formatting is idempotent. Layout is the
       formatter's job to change, and a guard that froze it would fail on every legitimate improvement
       and train the next reader to re-bless it.

       No spec case can reach this. A spec case is Maxon source that the runner COMPILES; the formatter
       is never invoked by the compile path, so the corpus is structurally blind to it - the same reason
       the four guards above exist. It compiles nothing and launches nothing (it formats six short
       strings in memory), so it costs one process. Chained AFTER the spec-run guard, keeping the six a
       defined sequence rather than two sharing an anchor. -->
  <Target Name="CheckFormatterPreservesComments" AfterTargets="CheckSpecRunVerdict">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; fmt-selftest" />
  </Target>

  <!-- THE SEVENTH GUARD. AArch64 reads encoding 31 as SP in the ADD/SUB immediate and extended-register
       forms and as XZR in the shifted-register form, so `sub sp, sp, x16` built from the wrong one
       assembles, disassembles, and moves nothing - the function's locals are then written over its
       caller's frame. Measured 2026-09-02: one function in an 18 MB self-compile had a 4096-byte locals
       area, one byte past what the immediate form can carry, and the compiler it built faulted on every
       program it was given.

       No spec case can pin it, and one that seemed to would be worse than none: the compiler picks
       frame sizes, so a case can only ask for one indirectly, and a case tuned to land on the boundary
       stops landing on it the moment anything upstream changes a slot - then passes forever while
       testing nothing. It compiles nothing and launches nothing (it emits twelve prologues in memory
       and decodes them), so it costs one process. Chained AFTER the formatter guard, keeping the seven a
       defined sequence rather than two sharing an anchor. -->
  <Target Name="CheckArm64StackPointerEncoding" AfterTargets="CheckFormatterPreservesComments">
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(ProjectDir)../bin/$(AssemblyName)$(_ExeExt)&quot; arm64-sp-selftest" />
  </Target>

</Project>